# What must never reach a commit here. The first three sections are the ordinary
# half. The last one is the reason this file is in the same issue as the
# attributes beside it rather than in a tidying change of its own.

# Build output. The command builds to a binary named for the module, and a cross
# build writes one per target.
/gegenprobe
/gegenprobe.exe
/dist/
*.exe
*.test

# What a measuring run leaves behind.
*.out
*.prof

# Run directories. A run writes a bundle and the raw output of every code it
# started, and a bundle can carry a host name, a user name, an absolute path or
# a case nobody has published yet. None of that is this repository's to hold,
# and the publish subcommand is the only route by which any of it is meant to
# leave a machine.
/run/
/runs/

# Operator supplied source. Several participating codes reach a machine only
# from a path the operator provides: one is distributed under terms allowing a
# single licensee and their research group to hold a copy, one states no terms
# at all, and one circulates in ports with none travelling alongside. Somebody
# following the operator documentation drops a copy beside the recipe that
# builds it, and a stray `git add -A` would then offer to redistribute a code
# this repository has no permission to carry. That is what this rule prevents,
# and it is why it is not tidiness.
recipes/**/source/
